COURSE_DATA["ins401"] = {"topics":[{"id":"t1","n":"Topic 1","title":"Introduction to Project Management","why":"The vocabulary for the whole course: project, PM, stakeholders, triple constraint, program vs portfolio, standards.","cards":[{"h":"What is a project?","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Definition — Project</b>A <b>temporary</b> endeavour (definite start and end) undertaken to create a <b>unique</b> product, service or result.</div>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Definition — Project management</b>The application of <b>knowledge, skills, tools and techniques</b> to project activities to meet the project requirements.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Project</th><th>Operations</th></tr><tr><td>Temporary, unique, time-bound</td><td>Ongoing, repetitive, business continuity</td></tr><tr><td>Building a new student portal</td><td>Running the portal every day</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Temporary does not mean short. A 5-year project is still temporary because it has an end.</div>"},{"h":"The project manager and stakeholders","b":"\n  <p><b>PM roles (lecturer):</b> liaison · team leadership · managing resources · communication · business objectives and benefits · managing constraints (scope, schedule, budget, quality) · managing risks.</p>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Definition — Stakeholder</b>A person, group or organisation that may <b>affect or be affected by</b> the project.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Internal</th><th>External</th></tr><tr><td>Sponsor, PMO, program manager, resource manager, PMs of other projects</td><td>Customers, end users, <b>shareholders</b>, regulatory bodies, suppliers</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trap\">On the lecturer's slides, shareholders are listed as <b>external</b>.</div>"},{"h":"Triple constraint · program vs portfolio","b":"\n  <p><b>Triple constraint:</b> scope, time, cost, with <b>quality in the centre</b>. Change one and the others move: add scope, and time and cost rise.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Program</th><th>Portfolio</th></tr><tr><td>A group of <b>related</b> projects managed together for benefits not available individually</td><td>Projects, programs <b>and operations</b> managed together to achieve <b>strategic objectives</b></td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Program = related. Portfolio = strategic (and can include operations).</div>"},{"h":"Standards, certifications and the PMI framework","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Key fact</th></tr><tr><td>PMI / PMBOK</td><td>USA; most comprehensive</td></tr><tr><td>PRINCE2</td><td>UK government origin; simpler, process-based</td></tr><tr><td>IPMA</td><td>Germany; competence-based</td></tr><tr><td>ISO 21500</td><td>International guidance standard</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Certifications:</b> CAPM → PMP → PgMP; PMI-ACP; PRINCE2 Foundation → Practitioner; MSP; IPMA levels D → A.</p>\n  <p><b>PMI framework:</b> 5 process groups (Initiating, Planning, Executing, Monitoring & Controlling, Closing) × 10 knowledge areas (Integration, Scope, Schedule, Cost, Quality, Resource, Communications, Risk, Procurement, Stakeholder).</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Knowledge areas: \"I Saw Six Cats Quietly Resting, Calmly Rubbing Paws Silently.\"</div>"},{"h":"Why project management matters","b":"\n  <p><b>Good PM gives:</b> clear objectives, efficient use of resources, risk control, on-time and on-budget delivery, stakeholder satisfaction, quality deliverables.</p>\n  <p><b>Poor PM causes:</b> missed deadlines, cost overruns, scope creep, poor quality and rework, unhappy stakeholders, failure (the lecturer cited abandoned projects in Nigeria).</p>\n  <p><b>Objective vs deliverable:</b> an objective is what the project aims to achieve (e.g. reduce registration time by 50%); a deliverable is a thing produced (the portal itself).</p>"}],"recall":[["Define a project.","A temporary endeavour undertaken to create a unique product, service or result."],["Define project management.","The application of knowledge, skills, tools and techniques to project activities to meet project requirements."],["Give two differences between a project and operations.","Projects are temporary and unique; operations are ongoing and repetitive (business continuity)."],["Define a stakeholder.","A person, group or organisation that may affect or be affected by the project."],["Are shareholders internal or external on the slides?","External."],["What sits at the centre of the triple constraint?","Quality (scope, time and cost are the sides)."],["Program vs portfolio?","Program: related projects for combined benefits. Portfolio: projects, programs and operations aligned to strategic objectives."],["Name the 5 PMI process groups.","Initiating, Planning, Executing, Monitoring & Controlling, Closing."]],"theory":[{"q":"Define a project and project management. Distinguish a project from operations, with examples.","pts":["Project definition: temporary + unique","PM definition: knowledge, skills, tools, techniques to meet requirements","Temporary vs ongoing","Unique vs repetitive","Example of each","Note: temporary ≠ short"],"model":"<p>A <b>project</b> is a temporary endeavour undertaken to create a unique product, service or result. <b>Project management</b> is the application of knowledge, skills, tools and techniques to project activities to meet the project requirements.</p><table><tr><th>Project</th><th>Operations</th></tr><tr><td>Temporary: definite start and end.</td><td>Ongoing with no planned end.</td></tr><tr><td>Unique output.</td><td>Repetitive output.</td></tr><tr><td>Ends when objectives are met.</td><td>Sustains the business (continuity).</td></tr><tr><td>E.g. building a hostel booking app.</td><td>E.g. the IT unit running the app daily.</td></tr></table><p>Temporary does not mean short: a five-year project is still temporary.</p>"},{"q":"Explain the triple constraint and distinguish a program from a portfolio.","pts":["Scope, time, cost named","Quality at the centre","Trade-off explained with example","Program definition","Portfolio definition (includes operations, strategic)","Clear contrast"],"model":"<p>The <b>triple constraint</b> links scope, time and cost, with quality at the centre. Changing one affects the others: adding features (scope) to a portal raises time and cost, or reduces quality if time and cost are fixed.</p><p>A <b>program</b> is a group of related projects managed in a coordinated way to obtain benefits not available from managing them individually (e.g. a university digitisation program: portal, e-library, payment system). A <b>portfolio</b> is a collection of projects, programs and operations managed together to achieve strategic objectives; the projects need not be related.</p>"}]},{"id":"t2","n":"Topic 2","title":"The Project Management Life Cycle","why":"Phases, development approaches (predictive/adaptive/hybrid), the SDLC, and EEFs vs OPAs (a favourite trap).","cards":[{"h":"The five life-cycle phases","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Initiation</span><em>→</em><span>Planning</span><em>→</em><span>Execution</span><em>→</em><span>Monitoring & Control</span><em>→</em><span>Closing</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Key work</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Initiation</td><td>Authorise the project; project charter; appoint the PM; high-level scope</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Planning</td><td>Project management plan (scope, schedule, cost, risk…)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Execution</td><td>Produce deliverables; acquire and manage resources</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Monitoring & Control</td><td>Track against baselines; validate scope; runs <b>alongside</b> execution</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Closing</td><td>Final report; lessons learned; release the team; hand over to operations</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Predictive vs adaptive vs hybrid","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Predictive (waterfall)</th><th>Adaptive (agile)</th><th>Hybrid</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Scope, time and cost fixed <b>early</b>; formal change control</td><td>Work in <b>iterations</b>; scope detailed per iteration; product backlog; high change</td><td>Predictive for fixed parts, adaptive for evolving parts</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Building a hostel</td><td>A new mobile app</td><td>E-library build (fixed) + ERP software (evolving)</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"product backlog\", \"iterations\" → adaptive · \"fixed early\", \"formal change control\" → predictive</div>"},{"h":"The SDLC (seven phases)","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Planning</span><em>→</em><span>Analysis</span><em>→</em><span>Design</span><em>→</em><span>Development</span><em>→</em><span>Testing</span><em>→</em><span>Deployment</span><em>→</em><span>Maintenance</span></div>\n  <ul><li><b>Planning:</b> feasibility</li><li><b>Analysis:</b> detailed requirements</li><li><b>Design:</b> layouts, <b>database design</b></li><li><b>Development</b> (= implementation): coding</li><li><b>Testing:</b> alpha (against design specs) and beta (with users)</li><li><b>Deployment:</b> release, training</li><li><b>Maintenance:</b> fix and improve; may loop back</li></ul>\n  <div class=\"trap\">CA1 asked: \"creating databases\" happens in which SDLC phase? The trap is Design (database <i>design</i>) vs Development (actually <i>creating</i> it). Read the verb.</div>"},{"h":"EEFs vs OPAs","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Enterprise Environmental Factors (EEFs)</b>Conditions <b>not under the control</b> of the project team that influence the project. Internal: culture, geographic distribution, <b>existing IT systems</b>, existing staff expertise. External: regulations, market, socio-cultural, financial (exchange rates).</div>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Organisational Process Assets (OPAs)</b>Plans, processes, policies, procedures (templates, change-control procedures) and knowledge bases (lessons learned, historical files) of the organisation.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Existing IT systems and existing staff skills are <b>internal EEFs</b>, NOT OPAs. \"Existing systems and culture\" was a CA1 option.</div>"}],"recall":[["Name the 5 project life-cycle phases.","Initiation, Planning, Execution, Monitoring & Control, Closing."],["What happens in Closing?","Final report, lessons learned, release the team, hand over to operations."],["Predictive vs adaptive life cycle?","Predictive: scope, time, cost fixed early with formal change control. Adaptive: iterations, scope defined per iteration, product backlog."],["When is a hybrid approach best?","When part of the project is fixed (predictive) and part is evolving (adaptive)."],["Name the 7 SDLC phases.","Planning, Analysis, Design, Development, Testing, Deployment, Maintenance."],["Alpha vs beta testing?","Alpha: against design specifications (internal). Beta: with real users."],["Define EEFs.","Conditions not under the control of the project team that influence the project (internal or external)."],["Are existing IT systems an EEF or OPA?","Internal EEF."]],"theory":[{"q":"Describe the five phases of the project management life cycle.","pts":["Initiation: charter, PM appointed","Planning: PM plan","Execution: deliverables, resources","M&C: track vs baselines, alongside execution","Closing: lessons learned, release team, handover","Logical order and brief example"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Initiation</b> — the project is authorised: business case reviewed, project charter issued, PM appointed, high-level scope set.</li><li><b>Planning</b> — the project management plan is developed: scope, schedule, cost, quality, risk, resources and communication.</li><li><b>Execution</b> — the team performs the work and produces deliverables; resources are acquired and managed.</li><li><b>Monitoring and controlling</b> — runs alongside execution: performance is tracked against baselines, changes are controlled, scope is validated.</li><li><b>Closing</b> — final report, lessons learned, team released, deliverables handed over to operations.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Distinguish between Enterprise Environmental Factors and Organisational Process Assets, giving examples of each.","pts":["EEF definition (not under team control)","Internal EEF examples","External EEF examples","OPA definition (processes/policies + knowledge bases)","OPA examples","Trap: existing IT/HR expertise = EEF"],"model":"<p><b>EEFs</b> are conditions not under the control of the project team that influence, constrain or direct the project. Internal: organisational culture, geographic distribution, existing IT systems, existing staff expertise. External: government regulations, market conditions, exchange rates, socio-cultural factors.</p><p><b>OPAs</b> are the organisation's own plans, processes, policies and procedures (templates, change-control procedures) and knowledge bases (lessons learned, historical project files).</p><p>Key distinction: an EEF is a <b>condition</b>; an OPA is a <b>document or record</b> the organisation owns. Existing IT systems are therefore an internal EEF, not an OPA.</p>"}]},{"id":"t3","n":"Topic 3","title":"Tools, Techniques and Processes","why":"ITTOs, the five process groups and their exceptions, tool vs technique, and the classic estimating techniques.","cards":[{"h":"Processes and ITTOs","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Process</b>A set of activities that turns <b>inputs</b> into <b>outputs</b> using <b>tools and techniques</b> (ITTO). An output becomes an input to another process or a deliverable.</div>\n  <p><b>Process groups:</b> Initiating (2 processes: <b>Develop Project Charter</b>, <b>Identify Stakeholders</b>) · Planning · Executing · Monitoring & Controlling · Closing (1: <b>Close Project or Phase</b>).</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Exceptions to the \"verb rule\": <b>Identify Stakeholders = Initiating</b>; Identify Risks = Planning; <b>Develop Team, Manage Quality, Acquire Resources = Executing</b>; Validate Scope, Perform Integrated Change Control = M&C. Scope, Schedule and Cost have <b>no</b> executing processes.</div>"},{"h":"Tool vs technique + key definitions","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Technique</th></tr><tr><td>Something <b>tangible</b>: template, software</td><td>A systematic <b>procedure</b> carried out by a person; may use tools</td></tr><tr><td>MS Project, a checklist template</td><td>Expert judgement, data gathering, inspection</td></tr></table>\n  <ul><li><b>Analogous estimating:</b> uses a similar past project</li><li><b>Parametric:</b> algorithm + parameters (cost per line of code)</li><li><b>Decomposition:</b> break into smaller parts</li><li><b>Prototypes:</b> working model for early feedback</li><li><b>Trend analysis:</b> forecast from past performance · <b>Variance analysis:</b> baseline vs actual · <b>Root cause analysis:</b> underlying reason</li><li><b>Earned value analysis:</b> combines scope, schedule and cost</li><li><b>Inspection:</b> examine whether work conforms to standards</li></ul>\n  <div class=\"trap\">CA1 asked which is NOT a technique: <b>scheduling software</b> (a tool).</div>"},{"h":"Work performance data → information → reports","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Data</span><em>(Direct & Manage Project Work)</em><em>→</em><span>Information</span><em>(controlling processes)</em><em>→</em><span>Reports</span><em>(Monitor & Control Project Work)</em></div>\n  <p><b>Direct and Manage Project Work</b> outputs: deliverables, work performance data, issue log, change requests. (CA1: the assumption log is NOT an output.)</p>\n  <p><b>PM software:</b> MS Project (enterprise), Asana (multiple views), ClickUp (sprints, flexible views), Trello (boards, lists, cards — Kanban), Jira (software teams, agile/scrum).</p>"}],"recall":[["What does ITTO stand for?","Inputs, Tools & Techniques, Outputs."],["Which 2 processes are in the Initiating group?","Develop Project Charter and Identify Stakeholders."],["Which process group has only one process?","Closing (Close Project or Phase)."],["Identify Stakeholders belongs to which process group?","Initiating."],["Tool vs technique?","A tool is tangible (template, software); a technique is a systematic procedure performed by a person."],["Analogous vs parametric estimating?","Analogous uses a similar past project; parametric uses an algorithm with parameters."],["Data → information → reports: which process produces work performance data?","Direct and Manage Project Work."],["Which PM tool uses boards, lists and cards (Kanban)?","Trello."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the five process groups and identify the exceptions students commonly get wrong.","pts":["Five groups named and described","Initiating: charter + identify stakeholders","Closing: one process","Identify Stakeholders = Initiating","Develop Team / Manage Quality / Acquire Resources = Executing","Validate Scope = M&C"],"model":"<p>PMI organises processes into five groups: <b>Initiating</b> (define and authorise: Develop Project Charter, Identify Stakeholders), <b>Planning</b> (establish scope, objectives and the plan), <b>Executing</b> (complete the work), <b>Monitoring and Controlling</b> (track, review and regulate performance) and <b>Closing</b> (Close Project or Phase — the only process).</p><p>Common exceptions: Identify Stakeholders is Initiating even though \"identify\" sounds like planning; Identify Risks is Planning; Develop Team, Manage Quality and Acquire Resources are Executing; Validate Scope and Perform Integrated Change Control are Monitoring and Controlling.</p>"},{"q":"Distinguish between a tool and a technique, and explain four techniques used in project management.","pts":["Tool definition (tangible)","Technique definition (procedure)","Technique 1 explained","Technique 2 explained","Technique 3 explained","Technique 4 explained"],"model":"<p>A <b>tool</b> is something tangible, such as a template or software program, used to produce an output. A <b>technique</b> is a defined, systematic procedure carried out by a person, which may use one or more tools.</p><ol><li><b>Expert judgement</b> — using people with specialised knowledge.</li><li><b>Analogous estimating</b> — estimating from a similar past project.</li><li><b>Parametric estimating</b> — using an algorithm and parameters (e.g. cost per square metre).</li><li><b>Variance analysis</b> — comparing baseline to actual performance to find deviations.</li></ol>"}]},{"id":"t4","n":"Topic 4","title":"Managing Project Teams I","why":"Resource processes, motivation, Goleman's six leadership styles and Tuckman's five stages.","cards":[{"h":"The three team processes","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Group</th><th>Key outputs</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Plan Resource Management</td><td>Planning</td><td>Resource management plan + <b>team charter</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Estimate Activity Resources</td><td>Planning</td><td>Resource requirements + <b>resource breakdown structure (RBS)</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Acquire Resources</td><td>Executing</td><td>Project team assignments + <b>resource calendars</b> (also an input)</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Team charter</b> = team values, agreements and operating guidelines. Different from the <b>project charter</b> (authorises the project).</p>\n  <p><b>Resource breakdown structure</b> = hierarchy of resources by <b>category and type</b> (labour, material, equipment, supplies).</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">CA1: the team charter is an output of <b>Plan Resource Management</b>.</div>"},{"h":"Motivating team members","b":"\n  <p>Ten strategies: open communication · clear goals and roles · timely conflict management · build trust · team-building · lead by example · collaborative decisions · celebrate small wins · training and tools · <b>tailored</b> recognition and rewards.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Tailored = different people are motivated by different rewards.</div>"},{"h":"Goleman's six leadership styles","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Style</th><th>Phrase</th><th>When</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Commanding</td><td>\"Do what I tell you\"</td><td>Crisis; use sparingly</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Visionary</td><td>\"Come with me\"</td><td>New vision / direction needed</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Affiliative</td><td>\"People come first\"</td><td>Heal rifts, motivate in stress</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Democratic</td><td>\"What do you think?\"</td><td>Build buy-in; slow, not for urgency</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Pacesetting</td><td>\"Do as I do, now\"</td><td>Quick results from a competent team; use sparingly</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Coaching</td><td>\"Try this\"</td><td>Long-term development; time-consuming</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Tuckman's five stages","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Forming</span><em>→</em><span>Storming</span><em>→</em><span>Norming</span><em>→</em><span>Performing</span><em>→</em><span>Adjourning</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Best style</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Forming</td><td>Get acquainted; ground rules</td><td>Visionary + coaching</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Storming</td><td>Conflict; members challenge roles</td><td>Affiliative + commanding</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Norming</td><td>Norms agreed; cohesion</td><td>Democratic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Performing</td><td>Optimal output</td><td>Democratic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Adjourning</td><td>Team disbands</td><td>—</td></tr></table>"}],"recall":[["What are the 2 key outputs of Plan Resource Management?","Resource management plan and team charter."],["What does the resource breakdown structure show?","Resources in a hierarchy by category and type."],["Which process group is Acquire Resources in?","Executing."],["Which Goleman style says \"Do as I do, now\"?","Pacesetting."],["Which style heals rifts (\"people come first\")?","Affiliative."],["Name Tuckman's five stages.","Forming, Storming, Norming, Performing, Adjourning."],["In which stage do members challenge roles?","Storming."],["Team charter vs project charter?","Team charter: team values and operating guidelines. Project charter: formally authorises the project."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain Tuckman's five stages of team development and the leadership style suitable for each.","pts":["Forming + style","Storming + style","Norming + style","Performing + style","Adjourning","Practical example"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Forming</b> — members meet and learn about the project; ground rules set. Visionary and coaching styles give direction.</li><li><b>Storming</b> — conflict as members compete and challenge roles. Affiliative style heals rifts; commanding if a crisis develops.</li><li><b>Norming</b> — norms agreed, trust builds. Democratic style invites participation.</li><li><b>Performing</b> — the team works at its best. Democratic style lets the team lead itself.</li><li><b>Adjourning</b> — work is complete and the team disbands; recognise contributions.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Describe Goleman's six leadership styles and when each is appropriate.","pts":["Commanding","Visionary","Affiliative","Democratic","Pacesetting","Coaching","\"Use sparingly\" for commanding and pacesetting"],"model":"<ul><li><b>Commanding</b> (\"Do what I tell you\") — crises; use sparingly.</li><li><b>Visionary</b> (\"Come with me\") — when a new vision or direction is needed.</li><li><b>Affiliative</b> (\"People come first\") — to heal rifts and motivate under stress.</li><li><b>Democratic</b> (\"What do you think?\") — to build buy-in; not ideal in urgent situations.</li><li><b>Pacesetting</b> (\"Do as I do, now\") — fast results from a highly competent team; use sparingly.</li><li><b>Coaching</b> (\"Try this\") — long-term development of people; time-consuming.</li></ul>"}]},{"id":"t5","n":"Topic 5","title":"Managing Project Teams II","why":"Power (French & Raven), conflict sources and strategies, virtual teams, communications management.","cards":[{"h":"Six sources of power","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Power</th><th>Based on</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Legitimate</td><td>Position or rank</td></tr><tr><td>Reward</td><td>Ability to give rewards</td></tr><tr><td>Coercive</td><td>Threat of punishment</td></tr><tr><td>Expert</td><td>Expertise / knowledge</td></tr><tr><td>Referent</td><td>Admiration, personal connection</td></tr><tr><td>Informational</td><td>Controlling access to information</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Expert = what you know. Informational = what information you control.</div>"},{"h":"Conflict: sources and five strategies","b":"\n  <p>Conflict is <b>inevitable</b> and not inherently good or bad. <b>Sources:</b> scarce resources · scheduling priorities · work styles · role/task ambiguity · poor communication · personality and values.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Avoiding</td><td>Withdraw or postpone</td></tr><tr><td>Accommodating</td><td>Concede to the other side</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Compromising</td><td>Middle ground; <b>lose-lose</b> on the slides</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Forcing / competing</td><td>Use power; suits emergencies</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Collaborating</td><td>Problem-solve; <b>win-win</b>, fully satisfies everyone</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Global and virtual teams","b":"\n  <p><b>Virtual team</b> = shared goal, little or no face-to-face contact (can still be local). <b>Global</b> = spread across countries.</p>\n  <p><b>Benefits:</b> wider talent pool, expertise, home-based workers, shifts across time zones, inclusion of people with disabilities, no travel or office costs.</p>\n  <p><b>Challenges:</b> language, time zones, tracking work, culture, knowledge gaps, labour laws.</p>\n  <p><b>Solutions:</b> clear channels, clear goals, flexible schedules, inclusivity, technology tools, regular check-ins.</p>"},{"h":"Communications management","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Key output</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Plan Communications Management</td><td>Communications management plan</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Manage Communications</td><td>Project communications (collect, create, distribute, store, retrieve)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Monitor Communications</td><td>Work performance information + change requests</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Enhancing communication:</b> active listening · writing style · meeting management · presentations · open communication and feedback · appropriate media.</p>"}],"recall":[["Name French and Raven's six power sources (as on the slides).","Legitimate, reward, coercive, expert, referent, informational."],["Power based on admiration?","Referent."],["Name 4 sources of conflict.","Any four: scarce resources, scheduling priorities, work styles, role ambiguity, poor communication, personality/values."],["Which strategy is win-win?","Collaborating."],["Which strategy is lose-lose on the slides?","Compromising."],["Avoiding vs accommodating?","Avoiding withdraws or postpones; accommodating concedes to the other party."],["Name the 3 communications processes.","Plan Communications Management, Manage Communications, Monitor Communications."],["Can a virtual team be local?","Yes. Virtual describes how they work (little face-to-face), not where."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain five strategies for managing conflict in project teams, stating when each is suitable.","pts":["Avoiding","Accommodating","Compromising (lose-lose)","Forcing (emergencies)","Collaborating (win-win)","Conflict is not inherently bad"],"model":"<p>Conflict is inevitable in teams and is not inherently good or bad; it must be managed.</p><ol><li><b>Avoiding</b> — withdrawing or postponing; suitable when the issue is trivial or tempers need to cool.</li><li><b>Accommodating</b> — conceding to the other party; suitable when harmony matters more than the issue.</li><li><b>Compromising</b> — each side gives up something (lose-lose on the slides); suitable when time is short.</li><li><b>Forcing/competing</b> — using power to impose a solution; suitable in emergencies.</li><li><b>Collaborating</b> — working together for a solution that fully satisfies everyone (win-win); best when time allows and commitment matters.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Discuss the benefits and challenges of virtual teams, and suggest ways to make them effective.","pts":["Definition","3+ benefits","3+ challenges","3+ solutions","Example"],"model":"<p>A <b>virtual team</b> shares a goal but has little or no face-to-face contact.</p><p><b>Benefits:</b> access to a wider talent pool and expertise; home-based workers; work across time zones in shifts; inclusion of people with disabilities; lower travel and office costs.</p><p><b>Challenges:</b> language barriers, time-zone differences, difficulty tracking work, cultural differences, knowledge gaps and differing labour laws.</p><p><b>Solutions:</b> clear communication channels, clear goals, flexible schedules, inclusive practices, collaboration tools (Jira, Slack, Teams) and regular check-ins.</p>"}]},{"id":"t6","n":"Topic 6","title":"Project Initiation and Planning","why":"How organisations choose projects, the business case, the charter, and the project management plan.","cards":[{"h":"Why organisations start projects","b":"\n  <p>Four factors: (1) <b>regulatory, legal or social</b> requirements; (2) <b>stakeholder requests</b>; (3) <b>strategic</b> opportunities or business needs; (4) to <b>create, improve or fix</b> products, processes or services.</p>"},{"h":"Pre-project documents","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Needs assessment</span><em>→</em><span>Business case</span><em>→</em><span>Benefits management plan</span><em>→</em><span>Project charter</span><em>→</em><span>PM plan</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th>Document</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Needs assessment</td><td>Identify problems/opportunities (SWOT, PESTEL)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Business case</td><td>Justify the project: options incl. <b>\"do nothing\"</b>, ROI, <b>payback period</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Benefits management plan</td><td>How and when benefits are delivered: <b>benefits owner</b>, metrics, timeframe</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"The project charter","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project charter</b>The document issued by the <b>sponsor</b> that <b>formally authorises</b> the project and gives the PM authority to apply organisational resources.</div>\n  <p>Contains (high level): purpose, objectives and success criteria, high-level requirements and scope, risks, milestone schedule, budget, stakeholders, exit criteria, PM name and authority, sponsor.</p>\n  <p><b>Initiating group:</b> Develop Project Charter (→ charter) + Identify Stakeholders (→ stakeholder register).</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">CA1: the sponsor does NOT develop the team charter (the team does).</div>"},{"h":"The project management plan","b":"\n  <p>Describes how the project will be <b>executed, monitored, controlled and closed</b>. It integrates all subsidiary plans (scope, schedule, cost, quality, resource, communications, risk, procurement, stakeholder) and the <b>baselines</b> (scope, schedule, cost → together the performance measurement baseline).</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Charter</th><th>PM plan</th></tr><tr><td>High level; authorises</td><td>Detailed; how the work will be done</td></tr><tr><td>Initiating</td><td>Planning</td></tr></table>"}],"recall":[["Name the four factors that lead organisations to start projects.","Regulatory/legal/social requirements; stakeholder requests; strategy; create, improve or fix products/processes."],["Which document includes the \"do nothing\" option and payback period?","The business case."],["Who owns the benefits in the benefits management plan?","The benefits owner."],["Who issues the project charter?","The sponsor."],["What does the charter do?","Formally authorises the project and gives the PM authority to use resources."],["Charter vs PM plan?","Charter: high-level authorisation (Initiating). PM plan: detailed how-to with baselines (Planning)."],["What three baselines make up the performance measurement baseline?","Scope, schedule and cost baselines."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the documents produced before and during project initiation, from needs assessment to the project charter.","pts":["Needs assessment (SWOT/PESTEL)","Business case (do nothing, ROI, payback)","Benefits management plan (owner, metrics, timeframe)","Charter: sponsor, authorises, PM authority","Correct order","Example"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Needs assessment</b> — identifies the problem or opportunity using tools such as SWOT and PESTEL.</li><li><b>Business case</b> — justifies the investment: compares options including \"do nothing\", and estimates ROI and payback period.</li><li><b>Benefits management plan</b> — states how and when benefits will be delivered and measured, the benefits owner, metrics and timeframe.</li><li><b>Project charter</b> — issued by the sponsor; formally authorises the project, names the PM and their authority, and records high-level objectives, scope, risks, milestones, budget and exit criteria.</li></ol><p>The PM plan then details how the project will be executed, monitored and controlled.</p>"}]},{"id":"t7","n":"Topic 7","title":"Managing Project Scope","why":"Scope is the fence around the project. Everything else (schedule, cost, risk, quality) is built on it.","cards":[{"h":"The big idea","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Definition — Project Scope Management</b>The processes required to ensure that the project includes <u>all the work required, and only the work required</u>, to complete the project successfully. It is primarily concerned with <b>defining and controlling</b> what is and is not included in the project.</div>\n  <p><b>\"All\"</b> protects you from <i>missing</i> work. <b>\"Only\"</b> protects you from <i>extra</i> work (scope creep). Students forget \"only\".</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Project scope</th><th>Product scope</th></tr><tr><td>The <b>work</b> done to deliver the product</td><td>The <b>features and functions</b> of the product itself</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Scope = the fence. Inside the fence: do it. Outside: don't, unless it's formally changed.</div>"},{"h":"The 6 scope processes (in order)","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Plan Scope Mgmt</span><em>→</em><span>Collect Requirements</span><em>→</em><span>Define Scope</span><em>→</em><span>Create WBS</span><em>→</em><span>Validate Scope</span><em>→</em><span>Control Scope</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Key output (learn these!)</th><th>Group</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Plan Scope Management</td><td>Scope management plan + Requirements management plan</td><td>Planning</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Collect Requirements</td><td>Requirements documentation + Requirements traceability matrix (RTM)</td><td>Planning</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Define Scope</td><td>Project scope statement</td><td>Planning</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Create WBS</td><td>Scope baseline</td><td>Planning</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Validate Scope</td><td>Accepted deliverables</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Control Scope</td><td>Work performance information + change requests</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>P</b>lease <b>C</b>ollect <b>D</b>ata, <b>C</b>reate <b>V</b>alid <b>C</b>ontrols\" → Plan, Collect, Define, Create WBS, Validate, Control.</div>"},{"h":"Plan Scope Management + Collect Requirements","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Plan Scope Management</b>The process of creating a scope management plan that documents how the project and product scope will be defined, validated and controlled.</div>\n  <p>Outputs: <b>scope management plan</b> (how scope will be defined, developed, monitored, controlled, validated) and <b>requirements management plan</b> (how requirements will be analysed, documented, managed). Both are \"HOW\" plans — neither lists actual requirements.</p>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Collect Requirements</b>The process of determining, documenting and managing stakeholder needs and requirements to meet objectives. It provides the basis for defining the product scope and project scope.</div>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> Data gathering — brainstorming, interviews, focus groups, questionnaires & surveys (benchmarking on the tools slide) · Decision making — voting, multicriteria decision analysis · Data representation — affinity diagrams, mind mapping · Context diagram · <b>Prototypes</b> (working model for early feedback).</p>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b> Requirements documentation (what is required) and the <b>Requirements Traceability Matrix</b> — a grid linking each requirement <b>from its origin to the deliverable that satisfies it</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"grid linking requirements to deliverables\" → RTM</div>"},{"h":"Define Scope → Project Scope Statement","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Define Scope</b>The process of developing a detailed description of the project and product, describing the product, service or result boundaries and acceptance criteria.</div>\n  <p>Output: <b>Project scope statement</b> — the description of the project scope, major deliverables, assumptions and constraints. Four key components:</p>\n  <ul><li><b>Project scope description</b> (progressively elaborated — starts high level, gets detailed)</li><li><b>Project deliverables</b></li><li><b>Acceptance criteria</b> (conditions to be met before a deliverable is accepted — used later in Validate Scope)</li><li><b>Project exclusions</b> (what is OUT of scope)</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Why exclusions matter:</b> stating what is out of scope manages stakeholder expectations and reduces scope creep. E.g. \"The mobile app is not included.\"</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Requirements documentation (needs) ≠ Scope statement (agreed boundary) ≠ WBS (work broken down).</div>"},{"h":"Create WBS → Scope Baseline","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Create WBS</b>The process of subdividing project deliverables and project work into smaller, more manageable components. It provides a framework of what has to be delivered.</div>\n  <p>Tool: <b>Decomposition</b>. Levels: Project → major deliverables/phases → sub-deliverables → <b>work packages</b> (lowest level).</p>\n  <ul><li>Each work package has a <b>unique ID</b> (e.g. 1.2.1) → forms a <b>code of accounts</b> for summing costs, schedule and resources.</li>\n  <li>Includes <b>all</b> work, including project management work (100% rule).</li>\n  <li><b>Scope baseline</b> = approved scope statement + WBS + WBS dictionary. Changed <b>only through formal change control</b>; used as the basis for comparison.</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's example (software project):</b> 1 Planning · 2 Analysis · 3 Design (3.1 Architecture, 3.2 Database, 3.3 Interface) · 4 Development (4.1 Frontend, 4.2 Backend, 4.3 Database) · 5 Testing (unit, integration, UAT) · 6 Deployment (deploy, training, monitoring).</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">WBS = <b>WHAT</b> work. Schedule = <b>WHEN</b>. A WBS has no dates.</div>"},{"h":"Validate Scope vs Control Scope","b":"\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Validate Scope</th><th>Control Scope</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Definition</td><td>Formalizing <b>acceptance</b> of the completed project deliverables</td><td><b>Monitoring</b> the status of project and product scope and <b>managing changes</b> to the scope baseline</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Who</td><td>Customer / sponsor signs off</td><td>Project team, throughout</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Key tool</td><td><b>Inspection</b> (examining deliverables against acceptance criteria)</td><td><b>Variance analysis</b> (baseline vs actual) and trend analysis</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Key input</td><td>Verified deliverables (from Control Quality)</td><td>Work performance data</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Key output</td><td><b>Accepted deliverables</b>, WPI, change requests</td><td>Work performance information, change requests</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Work performance data</b> = raw (\"12 of 30 done\"). <b>Work performance information</b> = analysed against baseline (\"40% done vs 55% planned — behind\").</p>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"formal acceptance by the customer\" → Validate Scope · \"compare baseline to actual\" → Control Scope</div>"},{"h":"Scope creep","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Scope creep</b>The uncontrolled expansion to project or product scope without adjustments to time, cost and resources.</div>\n  <p><b>Consequences:</b> budget overruns, missed deadlines, reduced quality.</p>\n  <p><b>Control measures (lecturer):</b></p>\n  <ol><li><b>Clear scope definition</b> — scope statement with explicit exclusions; the yardstick for \"in or out\".</li><li><b>Change control process</b> — every change request goes through Perform Integrated Change Control for review and approval.</li></ol>\n  <p><b>Example:</b> A client casually asks the developer to \"add one more report\"; the developer agrees without telling the PM → creep. If it's logged, assessed and approved with new time/cost → <b>approved change, NOT creep</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Creep = growing without permission.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Scope creep also appears as a <b>scheduling problem</b> (Topic 8) and a <b>scope risk</b> (Topic 10). Use it to link topics in essay answers.</div>"}],"recall":[["Define project scope management.","The processes required to ensure the project includes all the work required, and only the work required, to complete the project successfully; concerned with defining and controlling what is and is not included."],["Name the 6 scope management processes in order.","Plan Scope Management, Collect Requirements, Define Scope, Create WBS, Validate Scope, Control Scope."],["What is a Requirements Traceability Matrix?","A grid that links product requirements from their origin to the deliverables that satisfy them (output of Collect Requirements)."],["What are the 4 key components of the project scope statement?","Project scope description (progressively elaborated), project deliverables, acceptance criteria, project exclusions."],["What makes up the scope baseline?","The approved project scope statement + WBS + WBS dictionary. Changed only through formal change control."],["What is the lowest level of a WBS called?","Work package."],["Main tool of Create WBS?","Decomposition."],["Main tool of Validate Scope? Its key output?","Inspection. Accepted deliverables."],["Main tool of Control Scope?","Variance analysis (comparing baseline to actual), plus trend analysis."],["Define scope creep + 2 control measures.","Uncontrolled expansion of project/product scope without adjustments to time, cost and resources. Controls: clear scope definition (incl. exclusions) and a change control process."]],"theory":[{"q":"Define project scope management and briefly explain its six processes.","pts":["Definition with \"all the work required, and only the work required\"","Mentions defining and controlling what is/is not included","Plan Scope Management → scope mgmt plan + requirements mgmt plan","Collect Requirements → requirements documentation + RTM","Define Scope → project scope statement","Create WBS → decomposition, work packages, scope baseline","Validate Scope → formal acceptance, accepted deliverables","Control Scope → monitor status, manage changes to scope baseline"],"model":"<p><b>Project scope management</b> is the set of processes required to ensure that the project includes all the work required, and only the work required, to complete it successfully. It is concerned with defining and controlling what is and is not included in the project.</p><ol><li><b>Plan Scope Management</b> — creates the scope management plan (how scope will be defined, validated and controlled) and the requirements management plan.</li><li><b>Collect Requirements</b> — determines, documents and manages stakeholder needs using interviews, focus groups, surveys, prototypes etc.; outputs requirements documentation and the requirements traceability matrix.</li><li><b>Define Scope</b> — develops a detailed description of the project and product; output is the project scope statement (description, deliverables, acceptance criteria, exclusions).</li><li><b>Create WBS</b> — subdivides deliverables and work into smaller, manageable components (work packages) by decomposition; output is the scope baseline.</li><li><b>Validate Scope</b> — formalizes acceptance of completed deliverables by the customer/sponsor through inspection; output is accepted deliverables.</li><li><b>Control Scope</b> — monitors the status of scope and manages changes to the scope baseline using variance analysis.</li></ol><p>Together these prevent both missing work and scope creep.</p>"},{"q":"What is scope creep? Explain its consequences and two ways a project manager can control it, with an example.","pts":["Definition: uncontrolled expansion…without adjusting time, cost, resources","Consequences: budget overrun, missed deadlines, reduced quality","Control 1: clear scope definition / scope statement with exclusions","Control 2: change control process (Perform Integrated Change Control)","Relevant example","Distinguishes creep from an approved change"],"model":"<p><b>Scope creep</b> is the uncontrolled expansion of project or product scope without adjustments to time, cost and resources.</p><p><b>Consequences:</b> budget overruns, missed deadlines and reduced quality, because extra work is absorbed without extra time or money.</p><p><b>Controls:</b> (1) <b>Clear scope definition</b> — a detailed scope statement with explicit exclusions so every request can be judged as in or out of scope. (2) <b>Change control process</b> — all change requests go through Perform Integrated Change Control to be reviewed, approved or rejected, with time and cost adjusted.</p><p><b>Example:</b> during a school portal project a lecturer asks a developer to add an attendance report; the developer adds it informally, delaying testing. That is creep. If it had been logged as a change request and approved with a revised schedule, it would be a managed change, not creep.</p>"},{"q":"Differentiate between Validate Scope and Control Scope.","pts":["Validate = formal acceptance of completed deliverables","Validate done by customer/sponsor; tool inspection","Validate output: accepted deliverables","Control = monitor scope status + manage changes to scope baseline","Control tool: variance (and trend) analysis","Control outputs: work performance information, change requests"],"model":"<table><tr><th>Validate Scope</th><th>Control Scope</th></tr><tr><td>Process of formalizing acceptance of completed project deliverables.</td><td>Process of monitoring the status of project and product scope and managing changes to the scope baseline.</td></tr><tr><td>Focus: acceptance by customer/sponsor.</td><td>Focus: keeping the scope baseline intact; preventing creep.</td></tr><tr><td>Tool: inspection (and voting).</td><td>Tools: variance analysis, trend analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Input: verified deliverables from Control Quality.</td><td>Input: work performance data.</td></tr><tr><td>Outputs: accepted deliverables, WPI, change requests.</td><td>Outputs: WPI, change requests, plan updates.</td></tr></table>"}]},{"id":"t8","n":"Topic 8","title":"Managing Project Scheduling","why":"Scheduling turns the WBS into dates. CPM, PERT and crashing vs fast tracking are the most testable parts.","cards":[{"h":"Definition + 6 processes","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project Schedule Management</b>The processes required to manage the timely completion of the project. Scheduling provides a detailed plan that represents <b>how and when</b> the project will deliver the products, services and results defined in the project scope.</div>\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Plan Schedule Mgmt</span><em>→</em><span>Define Activities</span><em>→</em><span>Sequence Activities</span><em>→</em><span>Estimate Durations</span><em>→</em><span>Develop Schedule</span><em>→</em><span>Control Schedule</span></div>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>P</b>lease <b>D</b>on't <b>S</b>top <b>E</b>ating <b>D</b>elicious <b>C</b>ake.\"</div>\n  <p><b>Plan Schedule Management</b> → <b>schedule management plan</b> (policies, procedures, criteria for developing, monitoring and controlling the schedule). It is the rules, NOT the timetable.</p>"},{"h":"Define Activities","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Define Activities</b>The process of identifying and documenting the specific actions to be performed to produce the project deliverables. It <b>decomposes work packages into activities</b>.</div>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> expert judgement, decomposition, <b>rolling wave planning</b> (near-term work planned in detail, later work at a higher level), meetings.</p>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b></p><ul><li><b>Activity list</b> — every activity with an ID and scope of work description</li><li><b>Activity attributes</b> — extend each activity (ID, WBS ID, name, predecessors/successors…)</li><li><b>Milestone list</b> — significant points or events. <b>Milestones have zero duration.</b></li></ul>\n  <p>Example: work package \"Database design\" → activities: draft ER diagram, normalise tables, review with DBA.</p>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"zero duration\" → milestone</div>"},{"h":"Sequence Activities — PDM","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Sequence Activities</b>The process of identifying and documenting relationships among project activities; it defines the logical sequence of work for the greatest efficiency. Output: <b>project schedule network diagram</b>.</div>\n  <p>Tool: <b>Precedence Diagramming Method (PDM)</b>. A = predecessor, B = successor:</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>FS</b> Finish-to-Start (most common)</td><td>B can't start until A finishes</td><td>Code login → then test login</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>SS</b> Start-to-Start</td><td>B can't start until A starts</td><td>Write test cases once coding starts</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>FF</b> Finish-to-Finish</td><td>B can't finish until A finishes</td><td>Documentation finishes when coding finishes</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>SF</b> Start-to-Finish (rare)</td><td>B can't finish until A starts</td><td>Old system can't shut down until new one starts</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Lead</b> = successor starts early (acceleration). <b>Lag</b> = waiting time (delay), e.g. wait 3 days for concrete to cure.</p>"},{"h":"Estimate Activity Durations","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Estimate Activity Durations</b>The process of estimating the number of work periods needed to complete individual activities with estimated resources.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Idea</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Analogous</td><td>Use a <b>similar past</b> project/activity. Quick, less accurate.</td><td>Last portal took 6 months → ~6 months</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Parametric</td><td><b>Algorithm/statistical relationship</b> with a variable</td><td>100 lines/day × 5,000 lines → 50 days</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Three-point</td><td>Optimistic, most likely, pessimistic (basis of PERT)</td><td>O=2, M=3, P=4</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Bottom-up</td><td>Estimate lowest level, <b>add upward</b>. Most accurate, slowest.</td><td>Sum of activity estimates</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b> duration estimates + <b>basis of estimates</b> (how the estimate was derived). Also reserve analysis is a tool.</p>"},{"h":"Develop Schedule + Gantt","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Develop Schedule</b>The process of analyzing activity sequences, durations, resource requirements and schedule constraints to create a schedule model for project execution and monitoring and controlling.</div>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b></p><ul><li><b>Schedule baseline</b> — the <b>approved</b> version of the schedule model; changed only through formal change control; used to compare against actual results.</li><li><b>Project schedule</b> — linked activities with planned dates, durations, milestones and resources.</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> schedule network analysis, <b>critical path method</b>, resource optimization, what-if scenario analysis, simulation, leads & lags, <b>schedule compression</b>, PMIS, agile release planning.</p>\n  <p><b>Gantt chart:</b> bar chart on a calendar showing task dependencies, durations and critical path. (Network diagram = logic; Gantt = timeline.)</p>"},{"h":"Critical Path Method (CPM) — worked","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>CPM</b>A technique used to estimate the minimum project duration and determine the amount of schedule flexibility (float) on the logical network paths.</div>\n  <p><b>Critical path = the LONGEST path</b> through the network = the <b>shortest possible project duration</b>. Usually zero float. Delay a critical activity → the whole project is delayed.</p>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's school example</b> (buy & install computers + solar):</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Days</th><th>After</th></tr><tr><td>A Budget</td><td>2</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>B Purchase</td><td>4</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>C Install computers</td><td>1</td><td>B</td></tr><tr><td>D Install solar</td><td>3</td><td>B</td></tr><tr><td>E Test computers</td><td>1</td><td>C</td></tr><tr><td>F Test solar</td><td>2</td><td>D</td></tr><tr><td>G Deploy</td><td>1</td><td>E, F</td></tr></table>\n  <p>Path A–B–C–E–G = 2+4+1+1+1 = <b>9</b> · Path A–B–D–F–G = 2+4+3+2+1 = <b>12 ← critical</b>. Float on C and E = 12 − 9 = <b>3 days</b>.</p>\n  <p><b>Steps:</b> forward pass → ES, EF · backward pass → LS, LF · <b>Total Float = LS − ES = LF − EF</b> · activities with TF = 0 form the critical path.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Critical path is the LONGEST path, not the shortest. Crashing a non-critical task does NOT shorten the project.</div>"},{"h":"PERT — worked","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>PERT</b>A framework using three time estimates per task — optimistic (O), most likely (M), pessimistic (P) — to handle uncertain timelines and find the critical path.</div>\n  <p style=\"font-size:22px;text-align:center\"><b>E = (O + 4M + P) ÷ 6</b></p>\n  <p>Lecturer's example, same school project: A (2,3,4) → 3.0 · B (3,4,6) → 4.2 · D (2,3,5) → 3.2 · F (2,3,4) → 3.0 · G (1,2,3) → 2.0. Critical path A–B–D–F–G = <b>15.4 days</b> (other path 13.2).</p>\n  <table><tr><th>CPM</th><th>PERT</th></tr><tr><td>One fixed duration per activity</td><td>Three estimates, weighted average</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty not modelled</td><td>Uncertainty modelled</td></tr><tr><td>Known, repeated work</td><td>New / uncertain work</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">The Most likely counts 4 times: \"four in the middle\".</div>"},{"h":"Control Schedule + common problems","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Control Schedule</b>The process of monitoring the status of the project to update the project schedule and managing changes to the schedule baseline.</div>\n  <p>Outputs: <b>work performance information</b>, <b>schedule forecasts</b>, <b>change requests</b>. Tools: variance analysis, earned value analysis, iteration burndown chart, trend analysis, CPM, compression, resource optimization.</p>\n  <h3>Common scheduling problems → controls</h3>\n  <table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Control</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Scope creep (unplanned scope changes)</td><td>Clear scope statement + change control</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Resource management issues</td><td>Resource optimization / allocation</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Tracking tasks & deadlines</td><td>Project management software</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Communication inadequacies</td><td>A broad communication plan</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Unrealistic deadlines (wrong assumptions)</td><td>Better estimation; schedule compression</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Schedule compression: crashing vs fast tracking","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Schedule compression</b>Techniques used to shorten the schedule duration <b>without reducing the project scope</b>, to meet schedule constraints or imposed dates.</div>\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Crashing</th><th>Fast tracking</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>How</td><td><b>Add resources</b> (overtime, extra staff, pay to expedite) for the least incremental cost</td><td>Do activities normally done in sequence <b>in parallel</b> (overlap)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Side effect</td><td><b>Cost ↑</b></td><td><b>Risk ↑</b> and possible rework</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Example</td><td>Hire 2 extra developers for coding</td><td>Start front-end before database design is fully finished</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">CRASH = throw money/people at it. FAST-TRACK = overlap.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Cutting features is scope reduction, not compression. Compression only works on the critical path.</div>"}],"recall":[["Define project schedule management.","The processes required to manage the timely completion of the project; scheduling gives a detailed plan of how and when the project will deliver what is defined in the scope."],["Name the 6 schedule processes.","Plan Schedule Management, Define Activities, Sequence Activities, Estimate Activity Durations, Develop Schedule, Control Schedule."],["What are the 3 main outputs of Define Activities?","Activity list, activity attributes, milestone list."],["What is the duration of a milestone?","Zero."],["Name the 4 PDM relationships. Which is most common?","Finish-to-Start (most common), Start-to-Start, Finish-to-Finish, Start-to-Finish (rarest)."],["What is the critical path?","The longest path through the network; it gives the shortest possible project duration; activities on it usually have zero float."],["Formula for total float?","TF = LS − ES = LF − EF."],["PERT formula?","E = (O + 4M + P) / 6."],["Crashing vs fast tracking?","Crashing adds resources (cost rises). Fast tracking overlaps sequential activities (risk rises). Neither reduces scope."],["What is the schedule baseline?","The approved version of the schedule model, changed only through formal change control, used as the basis for comparison with actual results."],["Analogous vs parametric estimating?","Analogous uses a similar past project. Parametric uses a statistical relationship/algorithm with parameters (e.g. lines of code per day)."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the Critical Path Method. Using an example, show how the critical path and float are determined.","pts":["Definition: minimum project duration + float","Critical path = longest path = shortest project duration","Zero float on critical path; delay delays project","Forward pass (ES/EF) and backward pass (LS/LF)","TF = LS − ES or LF − EF","Worked example with paths summed correctly","Float of non-critical path calculated"],"model":"<p>The <b>Critical Path Method</b> is a technique used to estimate the minimum project duration and determine the amount of schedule flexibility (float) on the network paths. The <b>critical path</b> is the longest path through the network, so it gives the shortest possible time to finish the project. Activities on it normally have zero total float: any delay to them delays the project.</p><p><b>Steps:</b> list activities, durations and predecessors; draw the network; forward pass to find early start/finish; backward pass to find late start/finish; total float = LS − ES (or LF − EF); activities with zero float form the critical path.</p><p><b>Example:</b> A(2) → B(4) → C(1) → E(1) → G(1) and B → D(3) → F(2) → G. Path A–B–C–E–G = 9 days; A–B–D–F–G = 12 days. The critical path is A–B–D–F–G and the project takes 12 days. C and E have 12 − 9 = 3 days of float.</p>"},{"q":"Differentiate between crashing and fast tracking as schedule compression techniques.","pts":["Compression = shorten duration without reducing scope","Crashing = add resources, least incremental cost","Crashing increases cost","Fast tracking = sequential activities done in parallel","Fast tracking increases risk/rework","Example of each","Both applied to the critical path"],"model":"<p><b>Schedule compression</b> shortens schedule duration without reducing project scope.</p><table><tr><th>Crashing</th><th>Fast tracking</th></tr><tr><td>Shortens duration for the least incremental cost by adding resources: overtime, extra people, paying to expedite delivery.</td><td>Activities or phases normally done in sequence are performed in parallel for at least part of their duration.</td></tr><tr><td>Increases cost.</td><td>Increases risk and possible rework.</td></tr><tr><td>E.g. hiring two extra developers.</td><td>E.g. starting front-end coding before database design is complete.</td></tr></table><p>Both must be applied to critical-path activities; compressing a non-critical activity does not shorten the project.</p>"},{"q":"Discuss five common problems in project scheduling and how each can be controlled.","pts":["Scope creep → clear scope + change control","Resource management issues → resource allocation/optimization","Tracking tasks and deadlines → PM software","Communication inadequacies → communication plan","Unrealistic deadlines → better estimates / compression","Explains each, not just lists"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Scope creep</b> — unplanned changes in scope add work without extra time. Control: a clear scope statement and a change control process.</li><li><b>Resource management issues</b> — inadequate or over-allocated resources delay tasks. Control: proper resource allocation and resource optimization (levelling/smoothing).</li><li><b>Tracking tasks and deadlines</b> — poor tracking leads to missed deadlines. Control: project management software (e.g. MS Project, Jira).</li><li><b>Communication inadequacies</b> — unclear or delayed information causes wrong or late work. Control: a broad communication plan.</li><li><b>Unrealistic deadlines</b> — based on wrong assumptions in estimates. Control: better estimation techniques and schedule compression (crashing, fast tracking).</li></ol>"},{"q":"With examples, explain the four types of logical relationships used in the Precedence Diagramming Method.","pts":["PDM used in Sequence Activities to build network diagram","FS with example (most common)","SS with example","FF with example","SF with example (rare)","Mentions leads/lags (bonus)"],"model":"<p>The <b>Precedence Diagramming Method</b> is used in Sequence Activities to build the project schedule network diagram. For a predecessor A and successor B:</p><ol><li><b>Finish-to-Start</b> — B cannot start until A finishes. Most common. E.g. test the login after coding it.</li><li><b>Start-to-Start</b> — B cannot start until A starts. E.g. writing test cases starts when coding starts.</li><li><b>Finish-to-Finish</b> — B cannot finish until A finishes. E.g. documentation cannot finish until coding finishes.</li><li><b>Start-to-Finish</b> — B cannot finish until A starts. Rare. E.g. the old system cannot be switched off until the new one starts.</li></ol><p>Leads (acceleration) and lags (delay) adjust these relationships.</p>"}]},{"id":"t9","n":"Topic 9","title":"Project Budgeting & Managing Project Resources","why":"Two halves: the money (estimate → budget) and the resources (types + levelling vs smoothing). Both have a diagram or table on the slides, which makes them likely questions.","cards":[{"h":"Cost management: 4 processes","b":"\n  <p>Project budgeting is carried out under <b>Project Cost Management</b>:</p>\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Plan Cost Mgmt</span><em>→</em><span>Estimate Costs</span><em>→</em><span>Determine Budget</span><em>→</em><span>Control Costs</span></div>\n  <p><b>Plan Cost Management</b> → <b>cost management plan</b>: how costs will be estimated, budgeted, managed, monitored and controlled. Inputs include the charter, schedule management plan and risk management plan.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Estimate = how MUCH each piece costs. Budget = add it all up and get it APPROVED.</div>"},{"h":"Estimate Costs","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Estimate Costs</b>The process of developing an approximation of the monetary resources needed to complete project work.</div>\n  <ul><li>Estimates are <b>quantitative assessments of the probable costs</b> to complete the work.</li>\n  <li>Cover <b>all resources</b>: labour, materials, equipment, services, facilities, plus cost of financing and inflation allowance.</li>\n  <li>Include <b>contingency</b> for identified risks.</li>\n  <li>Are <b>aggregated by work package</b> (from the WBS) → control accounts → whole project.</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> expert judgement, analogous, parametric, three-point, bottom-up estimating, reserve analysis, <b>cost of quality</b>, PMIS, voting.</p>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b> cost estimates + basis of estimates.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">One slide says estimates include management reserve; the budget slide says the cost baseline <b>excludes</b> it. In the exam use the budget rule: <b>Budget = Cost baseline + Management reserve</b>.</div>"},{"h":"Determine Budget","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Determine Budget</b>The process of aggregating the estimated costs of individual activities or work packages to establish an authorized cost baseline, used to monitor and control project performance.</div>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b></p>\n  <ul><li><b>Cost baseline</b> — the approved version of the <b>time-phased</b> project budget, <b>excluding any management reserves</b>; changed only through formal change control.</li>\n  <li><b>Project funding requirements</b> — forecast costs to be paid, derived from the cost baseline (total or periodic): projected expenditures plus anticipated liabilities.</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> cost aggregation, reserve analysis, historical information review, <b>funding limit reconciliation</b>, financing. Inputs include the <b>business case</b> and <b>benefits management plan</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"time-phased, excluding management reserve\" → cost baseline</div>"},{"h":"Budget components (the stack diagram)","b":"\n  <p>Build it from the bottom up:</p>\n  <div class=\"flow\" style=\"flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch\">\n   <span style=\"background:#5a3c0c\">PROJECT BUDGET = Cost baseline + Management reserve</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#7a5212\">Management reserve (unplanned work — unknown unknowns)</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#8a5a14\">Cost baseline</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#a06c1e\">Control accounts</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#b47f2a\">Work package cost estimates + contingency reserve</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#c99640\">Activity cost estimates + activity contingency reserve</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Contingency reserve</th><th>Management reserve</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>For</td><td><b>Identified</b> risks (known unknowns)</td><td><b>Unplanned</b> work (unknown unknowns)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Where</td><td><b>Inside</b> the cost baseline</td><td><b>Outside</b> the baseline, inside the budget</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Control</td><td>PM</td><td>Management approval needed</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Example:</b> work packages ₦60m + contingency ₦6m = cost baseline ₦66m; + management reserve ₦4m = project budget ₦70m.</p>"},{"h":"4 types of project resources","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project resources</b>The essential elements, both tangible and intangible, required to ensure successful completion of projects. Proper allocation, monitoring and control is crucial.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Includes</th><th>If badly managed</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Human</b></td><td>Project team led by the PM; full-time, part-time, specialists</td><td>Limited skilled staff, overallocation, poor training → delays, burnout, poor quality</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Physical</b></td><td>Equipment, materials, facilities, infrastructure</td><td>Missing equipment → delay; poor materials → recalls/rework; too much inventory → high cost; too little → shortages</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Financial</b></td><td>Funds for labour, equipment, materials, overhead</td><td>Insufficient funds → quality issues, delay, cancellation; overspend → budget overrun</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Time</b></td><td>Schedules and deadlines; estimation; tracking</td><td>Unrealistic deadlines, unclear timelines → rushed work, quality issues, unmet expectations</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">H-P-F-T: People, Property, Finance, Time.</div>"},{"h":"Resource levelling","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Resource levelling</b>A technique in which start and finish dates are adjusted based on resource constraints, with the goal of balancing the demand for resources with the available supply.</div>\n  <p>Used when key resources are available only at certain times, in limited quantities, or are <b>over-allocated</b>. It <b>may change the critical path</b> and often <b>extends</b> the project.</p>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's example:</b> A needs John + Jane, B needs Jane, both on Day 1, then C (John) on Day 2. Jane is booked 16 hrs on Day 1 → over-allocated. Levelling delays B to Day 2, C moves to Day 3. Nobody exceeds 8 hrs, but the project goes from <b>2 days to 3 days</b> and the critical path becomes A → B → C.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">LEVELLING: Limited resources → dates move → critical path may change.</div>"},{"h":"Resource smoothing + the comparison","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Resource smoothing</b>A technique that adjusts activities so resource requirements do not exceed predefined limits. It moves activities <b>within their float</b>, without changing the project duration or critical path, to get more <b>uniform</b> resource use.</div>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's example:</b> limit of 6 people on the whole project. Days 3–4 needed 7 (A: 5 + B: 2). B had free float, so its work was spread at 1 person/day over Days 2–6. Peak = 6, end date unchanged.</p>\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Levelling</th><th>Smoothing</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Objective</td><td>Balance demand with supply</td><td>Uniform resource utilization</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Critical path</td><td>Often <b>changes</b></td><td><b>Not changed</b>; uses float</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Main constraint</td><td><b>Critical resources</b></td><td><b>Project duration</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>When</td><td>Resources limited / available only at certain times / over-allocated</td><td>Limits on resource usage or uneven usage</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Dates</td><td>Start and end dates can change</td><td>Meets the initial deadline</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Smoothing needs float. If there's no float, it can't work; you must level (and accept a later end date).</div>"}],"recall":[["Name the 4 cost management processes.","Plan Cost Management, Estimate Costs, Determine Budget, Control Costs."],["Define Determine Budget.","Aggregating the estimated costs of individual activities or work packages to establish an authorized cost baseline."],["What is the cost baseline?","The approved version of the time-phased project budget, excluding management reserves; changed only through formal change control."],["Project budget formula?","Project Budget = Cost Baseline + Management Reserve."],["Contingency vs management reserve?","Contingency covers identified risks and is inside the cost baseline. Management reserve covers unplanned work and sits outside the baseline (inside the budget)."],["What are project funding requirements?","Forecast costs to be paid, derived from the cost baseline, for total or periodic needs: expenditures plus anticipated liabilities."],["4 types of project resources?","Human, physical, financial, time."],["Define resource levelling.","Adjusting start and finish dates based on resource constraints to balance resource demand with available supply; may change the critical path."],["Define resource smoothing.","Adjusting activities within their float so resource needs stay within predefined limits, without changing the critical path or project end date."],["Which one has project duration as the main constraint?","Smoothing. (Levelling: critical resources are the main constraint.)"]],"theory":[{"q":"With the aid of a diagram, explain the components of a project budget.","pts":["Activity cost estimates + activity contingency","Work package estimates + contingency reserve","Control accounts","Cost baseline (time-phased, excludes management reserve)","Management reserve for unplanned work","Budget = baseline + management reserve","Contingency (identified risks) vs management reserve distinguished","Diagram drawn bottom-up"],"model":"<p>The project budget is built up from the lowest level of cost:</p><ol><li><b>Activity cost estimates</b> plus an <b>activity contingency reserve</b> for identified risks.</li><li>These are aggregated into <b>work package cost estimates</b>, with a further <b>contingency reserve</b>.</li><li>Work packages are grouped into <b>control accounts</b> for management control.</li><li>Control accounts plus contingency make up the <b>cost baseline</b>: the approved, time-phased budget excluding management reserve, changed only through formal change control.</li><li>A <b>management reserve</b> is added for unplanned work (unknown unknowns). It is held by management and released only with approval.</li><li><b>Project budget = cost baseline + management reserve.</b></li></ol><p>[Draw the stack: activity estimates at the bottom → work packages → control accounts → cost baseline → management reserve → project budget at the top.]</p>"},{"q":"Differentiate between resource levelling and resource smoothing.","pts":["Levelling definition (adjust dates, balance demand and supply)","Smoothing definition (within float, uniform use)","Critical path: changes vs unchanged","Constraint: critical resources vs project duration","Dates/duration: may extend vs initial deadline kept","When each is used","Example"],"model":"<table><tr><th>Resource levelling</th><th>Resource smoothing</th></tr><tr><td>Adjusts start and finish dates based on resource constraints to balance demand for resources with available supply.</td><td>Adjusts activities within their float so requirements don't exceed predefined limits, giving uniform utilization.</td></tr><tr><td>Often changes the critical path.</td><td>Does not change the critical path; uses float.</td></tr><tr><td>Critical resources are the primary constraint.</td><td>Project duration is the primary constraint.</td></tr><tr><td>Used when resources are available only at certain times, in limited quantity, or over-allocated.</td><td>Used when there are limits on resource usage or usage is uneven.</td></tr><tr><td>Start and end dates may change (project may be extended).</td><td>Meets the initial deadline.</td></tr></table><p>E.g. levelling: one DBA booked on two parallel tasks → one task delayed, project ends later. Smoothing: a non-critical documentation task spread over its float to avoid a staffing peak.</p>"},{"q":"Explain the four types of project resources and the implications of poorly managing each.","pts":["Definition of project resources","Human + implications","Physical + implications","Financial + implications","Time + implications","Examples given"],"model":"<p><b>Project resources</b> are the tangible and intangible elements required to complete a project successfully.</p><ol><li><b>Human</b> — the team led by the PM. Poor management (limited skilled staff, overallocation, no training) leads to delays, burnout and poor quality.</li><li><b>Physical</b> — equipment, materials, facilities. Failing to secure equipment causes delays; poor materials cause recalls and rework; too much inventory raises costs, too little causes shortages.</li><li><b>Financial</b> — funds for labour, equipment and overheads. Insufficient funds cause quality problems, delay or cancellation; poor budgeting causes overruns.</li><li><b>Time</b> — schedules and deadlines. Unrealistic deadlines and unexpected delays cause rushed work, quality issues and unmet expectations.</li></ol>"}]},{"id":"t10","n":"Topic 10","title":"Managing Project Risk","why":"The biggest topic here. Know the definition, the 7 types, the 7 processes, the P×I matrix, EMV, and the 5 response strategies.","cards":[{"h":"What is a risk?","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project risk</b>An uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, can impact a project's objectives such as scope, budget, timeline or quality.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Positive risk (opportunity)</th><th>Negative risk (threat)</th></tr><tr><td>New technology lets you finish early; favourable interest rates</td><td>Supplier delays; key staff resign</td></tr></table>\n  <table><tr><th>Internal</th><th>External</th></tr><tr><td>Communication, resource availability</td><td>Regulatory changes, exchange/interest rates</td></tr></table>\n  <p>Risks vary in <b>likelihood</b> and <b>impact</b>; identify and prioritise them early.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Risk = MAYBE. If it's already happening, it's an <b>issue</b>.</div>"},{"h":"7 types of project risk","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Cause</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Scope</b></td><td>Requirements unclear, change unexpectedly, or expand without authorization (scope creep)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Schedule</b></td><td>Unrealistic timelines, resource shortages, ignoring the critical path, external dependencies</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Cost / financial</b></td><td>Costs exceed budget: poor estimation, unexpected expenses</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Resource</b></td><td>Lack of people, equipment, materials: overallocation, resignations, injuries, supply issues</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Operational</b></td><td>Internal processes, systems, team dynamics: poor communication, inadequate training</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Technical</b></td><td>Technology: bugs, hardware failure, outdated systems, e.g. migrating from servers to cloud → data loss, downtime</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>External</b></td><td>Outside team's control: regulation, market shifts, natural disasters (e.g. rainy season)</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>S</b>ix <b>S</b>mart <b>C</b>ats <b>R</b>un <b>O</b>ver <b>T</b>he <b>E</b>dge\" — Scope, Schedule, Cost, Resource, Operational, Technical, External.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Classify by <b>cause</b>: naira devaluing makes servers dearer → <b>external</b> (cost is just the effect).</div>"},{"h":"Risk management: 7 processes","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project risk management</b>The processes of conducting risk management planning, identification, analysis, response planning, response implementation and monitoring of risk. Objective: increase the probability/impact of positive risks and decrease the probability/impact of negative risks.</div>\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Plan Risk Mgmt</span><em>→</em><span>Identify Risks</span><em>→</em><span>Qualitative Analysis</span><em>→</em><span>Quantitative Analysis</span><em>→</em><span>Plan Responses</span><em>→</em><span>Implement Responses</span><em>→</em><span>Monitor Risks</span></div>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Plan → Find → Rank → Number → Respond → Do → Watch.</div>\n  <p><b>Plan Risk Management</b> — defines how to conduct risk management; makes the effort proportionate to the risks and the project's importance. Output: <b>risk management plan</b>. Tool: stakeholder analysis.</p>\n  <p>Risk management is <b>not a one-time task</b>: it's ongoing and iterative.</p>"},{"h":"Identify Risks: register vs report","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Identify Risks</b>The process of identifying individual project risks as well as sources of overall project risk, and documenting their characteristics.</div>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> brainstorming, checklists, interviews, root cause analysis, assumption & constraint analysis, <b>SWOT</b>, document analysis, prompt lists, facilitation.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Risk register</th><th>Risk report</th></tr><tr><td>Details of <b>individual</b> risks; potential owners and responses</td><td>Sources of <b>overall</b> project risk + summary (number of threats/opportunities, by category)</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Register columns (lecturer's template):</b> ID · date raised · description · likelihood · impact · severity · owner · mitigating action.</p>\n  <p>E.g. \"Exchange rate depreciation → budget overrun\" · Medium · High · High · Sponsor · contingency reserve + monitor rates.</p>"},{"h":"Qualitative analysis: P × I matrix","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Perform Qualitative Risk Analysis</b>The process of prioritizing individual project risks for further analysis or action by assessing their probability of occurrence and impact.</div>\n  <p><b>Risk score = Probability × Impact</b></p>\n  <table class=\"mono\"><tr><th>P ↓ / I →</th><th>.05</th><th>.10</th><th>.20</th><th>.40</th><th>.80</th></tr>\n  <tr><th>.90</th><td>.05</td><td>.09</td><td>.18</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.36</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.72</td></tr>\n  <tr><th>.70</th><td>.04</td><td>.07</td><td>.14</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.28</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.56</td></tr>\n  <tr><th>.50</th><td>.03</td><td>.05</td><td>.10</td><td>.20</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.40</td></tr>\n  <tr><th>.30</th><td>.02</td><td>.03</td><td>.06</td><td>.12</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.24</td></tr>\n  <tr><th>.10</th><td>.01</td><td>.01</td><td>.02</td><td>.04</td><td>.08</td></tr></table>\n  <p>Green ≈ ≤0.07 (low) · Yellow ≈ 0.08–0.20 (medium) · <b>Red ≥ 0.24 (high)</b>.</p>\n  <p>Tools: probability & impact assessment, P&I matrix, risk categorization, risk data quality assessment. Output: <b>updates</b> to the risk register/report.</p>"},{"h":"Quantitative analysis: EMV & decision tree","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Perform Quantitative Risk Analysis</b>Numerically analyzing the combined effect of identified risks and other uncertainty on overall project objectives. <b>Not required for every project.</b></div>\n  <p>Tools: <b>simulation</b>, <b>sensitivity analysis</b>, <b>decision tree analysis</b>, <b>influence diagrams</b>. Output: risk report update.</p>\n  <p><b>EMV = Probability × Impact</b>, summed over each outcome of an option.</p>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's example:</b> late penalty ₦20m. In-house costs ₦4m with 30% chance of default. Outsource costs ₦8m with 5% chance.</p>\n  <table class=\"mono\"><tr><th>Option</th><th>Working</th><th>EMV</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>In-house</td><td>0.30 × −24 + 0.70 × −4 = −7.2 − 2.8</td><td><b>−₦10m</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Outsource</td><td>0.05 × −28 + 0.95 × −8 = −1.4 − 7.6</td><td><b>−₦9m</b></td></tr></table>\n  <p>Choose <b>outsource</b> (lower expected cost), even though it's dearer upfront.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Qualitative</th><th>Quantitative</th></tr><tr><td>Prioritizes individual risks</td><td>Numerically measures overall exposure</td></tr><tr><td>Ratings (low/med/high)</td><td>Money, time, probabilities</td></tr><tr><td>Normally always done</td><td>Not required for every project</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Plan, Implement, Monitor + RBS","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Key tools/outputs</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Plan Risk Responses</b></td><td>Developing options, selecting strategies and agreeing actions for overall and individual risks</td><td>Strategies for threats/opportunities, contingent responses, cost-benefit analysis</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Implement Risk Responses</b></td><td>Implementing the agreed risk response plans</td><td>Influencing, PMIS</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Monitor Risks</b></td><td>Monitoring responses, tracking risks, identifying new ones, evaluating effectiveness</td><td>Technical performance analysis, reserve analysis, audits → WPI, change requests</td></tr></table>\n  <h3>Risk Breakdown Structure (RBS)</h3>\n  <p>A hierarchical representation of potential <b>sources</b> of risk. Level 1 categories:</p>\n  <p><span class=\"pill\">1 Technical</span><span class=\"pill\">2 Management</span><span class=\"pill\">3 Commercial</span><span class=\"pill\">4 External</span></p>\n  <p>E.g. Technical: scope definition, requirements, technology · Management: PM, resourcing, communication · Commercial: contracts, suppliers, customer stability · External: legislation, <b>exchange rates</b>, weather, competition.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">WBS breaks down <b>work</b>. RBS (risk) breaks down <b>sources of risk</b>.</div>"},{"h":"5 risk response strategies (threats)","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Avoid</b></td><td><b>Eliminate</b> the threat or protect the project from it, e.g. by changing the plan/scope</td><td>Use proven technology instead of a new one; extend schedule; reduce scope</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Mitigate</b></td><td><b>Reduce</b> likelihood or impact; early action beats repair</td><td>More stable supplier; more tests; regular backups</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Accept</b></td><td>Acknowledge, no proactive action. <b>Passive</b> (do nothing) or <b>active</b> (contingency reserve of time/money)</td><td>Extra time for weather delays</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Transfer</b></td><td>Shift ownership/impact to a <b>third party</b>, usually for a <b>risk premium</b></td><td>Insurance for shipped items</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Escalate</b></td><td>Threat is <b>outside project scope</b> or beyond the <b>PM's authority</b> → passed to sponsor/senior management</td><td>Technical hurdle risking reputation and the whole budget</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>A</b>ll <b>M</b>y <b>A</b>nswers <b>T</b>ake <b>E</b>ffort\": Avoid, Mitigate, Accept, Transfer, Escalate.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Avoid = risk can't happen. Mitigate = risk still possible but smaller. Transfer = third party outside; Escalate = up the chain inside the organization.</div>"}],"recall":[["Define project risk.","An uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, can impact a project's objectives such as scope, budget, timeline or quality. It can be positive (opportunity) or negative (threat)."],["Name the 7 types of project risk.","Scope, schedule, cost/financial, resource, operational, technical, external."],["Name the 7 risk management processes.","Plan Risk Management, Identify Risks, Perform Qualitative Risk Analysis, Perform Quantitative Risk Analysis, Plan Risk Responses, Implement Risk Responses, Monitor Risks."],["Risk register vs risk report?","Register: details of individual risks, owners and responses. Report: sources of overall project risk plus summary information."],["P = 0.7, I = 0.4. Score and band?","0.28 → high (red)."],["EMV formula?","EMV = Probability × Impact, summed over all outcomes of an option."],["4 quantitative analysis tools?","Simulation, sensitivity analysis, decision tree analysis, influence diagrams."],["Is quantitative analysis required for every project?","No."],["5 response strategies for threats?","Avoid, mitigate, accept, transfer, escalate."],["Passive vs active acceptance?","Passive: do nothing. Active: set aside a contingency reserve of time, money or resources."],["4 level-1 categories of the RBS?","Technical, management, commercial, external."],["Insurance is which strategy?","Transfer (to a third party, with a premium)."]],"theory":[{"q":"Define project risk and explain any five types of risk in projects, with examples.","pts":["Definition (uncertain event, if it occurs, impacts objectives)","Positive vs negative mentioned","Type 1 explained + example","Type 2 explained + example","Type 3 explained + example","Type 4 explained + example","Type 5 explained + example"],"model":"<p>A <b>project risk</b> is an uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, can impact a project's objectives such as scope, budget, timeline or quality. Risks may be negative (threats) or positive (opportunities).</p><ol><li><b>Scope risk</b> — requirements are unclear, change unexpectedly or expand without formal authorization (scope creep). E.g. a client keeps adding features.</li><li><b>Schedule risk</b> — the project may miss its planned schedule due to unrealistic timelines, resource shortages or ignoring the critical path.</li><li><b>Cost risk</b> — actual costs may exceed the budget due to poor estimation or unexpected expenses.</li><li><b>Resource risk</b> — the project may lack personnel, equipment or materials, e.g. a key developer resigns.</li><li><b>Technical risk</b> — technology problems such as software bugs or hardware failure, e.g. data loss when migrating from local servers to the cloud.</li><li>(Also operational: poor internal processes/communication; external: regulation, market shifts, weather.)</li></ol>"},{"q":"Explain the five risk response strategies for negative risks, with an example of each.","pts":["Avoid: eliminate/change plan + example","Mitigate: reduce probability/impact + example","Accept: passive vs active (contingency) + example","Transfer: third party, premium + example","Escalate: outside scope/authority → sponsor + example"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Avoidance</b> — acting to eliminate the threat or protect the project from its impact, e.g. by changing the plan or scope. E.g. using a proven technology instead of an untested one.</li><li><b>Mitigation</b> — reducing the probability or impact of the threat. E.g. choosing a more reliable supplier, adding more tests, keeping regular backups.</li><li><b>Acceptance</b> — acknowledging the threat without proactive action. Passive acceptance does nothing; active acceptance sets aside a contingency reserve. E.g. adding extra time for rainy-season delays.</li><li><b>Transfer</b> — shifting ownership and impact of the threat to a third party, usually for a risk premium. E.g. insuring equipment being shipped.</li><li><b>Escalation</b> — used when the threat is outside the project scope or beyond the PM's authority; it is passed to the sponsor or senior management. E.g. a technical hurdle that could cause reputational damage to the whole organization.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Differentiate between qualitative and quantitative risk analysis. Use an example to show how expected monetary value (EMV) supports a decision.","pts":["Qualitative: prioritizes individual risks by probability and impact","Qualitative tool: P&I matrix","Quantitative: numerically analyzes combined effect / overall exposure","Quantitative tools (simulation, sensitivity, decision tree, influence diagrams); not always required","EMV = P × I","Correct EMV calculation for two options","Correct decision stated"],"model":"<p><b>Qualitative risk analysis</b> prioritizes individual risks for further action by assessing probability and impact, using descriptive ratings and the probability-impact matrix (score = P × I). It is quick and normally done on all projects.</p><p><b>Quantitative risk analysis</b> numerically analyzes the combined effect of risks on overall project objectives, using simulation, sensitivity analysis, decision trees and influence diagrams. It is not required for every project.</p><p><b>EMV example:</b> a late-delivery penalty is ₦20m. In-house: cost ₦4m, 30% chance of delay. EMV = 0.3 × (−24) + 0.7 × (−4) = −₦10m. Outsource: cost ₦8m, 5% chance. EMV = 0.05 × (−28) + 0.95 × (−8) = −₦9m. Outsourcing has the lower expected cost, so it is chosen.</p>"},{"q":"Briefly explain the seven project risk management processes.","pts":["Plan Risk Management → risk management plan","Identify Risks → risk register + risk report","Qualitative → prioritize by P & I","Quantitative → numerical, overall exposure","Plan Risk Responses → strategies/actions","Implement Risk Responses → carry out plans","Monitor Risks → track, new risks, effectiveness","Ongoing/iterative"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Plan Risk Management</b> — defines how risk management will be conducted; output: risk management plan.</li><li><b>Identify Risks</b> — identifies individual risks and sources of overall risk; outputs: risk register and risk report.</li><li><b>Perform Qualitative Risk Analysis</b> — prioritizes risks by probability and impact.</li><li><b>Perform Quantitative Risk Analysis</b> — numerically analyzes the combined effect of risks (simulation, decision trees).</li><li><b>Plan Risk Responses</b> — develops options, selects strategies and agrees actions.</li><li><b>Implement Risk Responses</b> — carries out the agreed response plans.</li><li><b>Monitor Risks</b> — monitors responses, tracks risks, identifies new ones and evaluates effectiveness.</li></ol><p>Risk management is ongoing and iterative, not a one-time task.</p>"}]},{"id":"t11","n":"Topic 11","title":"Managing Project Quality","why":"Three processes, the Manage-vs-Control distinction, cost of quality, and four tools (fishbone, Pareto, benchmarking/checklists, Six Sigma).","cards":[{"h":"What is quality?","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Quality (PMBOK)</b>The degree to which a set of inherent characteristics fulfils requirements.</div>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project quality</b>The degree to which a project's deliverables and outcomes meet stakeholder requirements and expectations.</div>\n  <p>Quality is measured against <b>requirements</b>, not luxury. A simple app that does exactly what was asked with no bugs = high quality. A feature-packed app that crashes = low quality.</p>\n  <p>Quality sits in the <b>centre of the triple constraint</b>: squeezing scope, time or cost affects quality.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Quality = fit to requirements, not fancy.</div>"},{"h":"Quality management: 3 processes","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project Quality Management</b>The processes for incorporating the organization's quality policy regarding planning, managing and controlling project and product quality requirements, in order to meet stakeholder objectives.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Group</th><th>Outputs</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Plan Quality Management</b> — identify quality requirements/standards and document how compliance will be demonstrated</td><td>Planning</td><td>Quality management plan; quality metrics</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Manage Quality</b> — translate the quality plan into executable quality activities</td><td>Executing</td><td>Quality reports; test & evaluation documents; change requests</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Control Quality</b> — monitor and record results to ensure outputs are complete, correct and meet customer expectations</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling</td><td>Quality control measurements; <b>verified deliverables</b>; work performance information; change requests</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">P-M-C: <b>P</b>lan the standard, <b>M</b>anage the process, <b>C</b>ontrol the product.</div>"},{"h":"Plan Quality: plan vs metrics","b":"\n  <p><b>Quality management plan</b> — part of the PM plan; describes how policies, procedures and guidelines will be implemented to achieve quality objectives. (The HOW.)</p>\n  <p><b>Quality metric</b> — describes a project or product attribute and how Control Quality will verify compliance. (The WHAT you measure.) E.g. errors per line of code, failure rate, page loads in under 2 seconds.</p>\n  <p>Tools include <b>benchmarking</b>, cost-benefit analysis, <b>cost of quality</b>, brainstorming, interviews.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Change requests are NOT an output of Plan Quality Management.</div>"},{"h":"Manage Quality vs Control Quality","b":"\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Manage Quality (QA)</th><th>Control Quality (QC)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Focus</td><td><b>Process</b> — are we following the right procedures? Can they improve?</td><td><b>Product</b> — is this deliverable complete and correct?</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Group</td><td>Executing</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Tools</td><td>Audits, process analysis, root cause analysis, quality improvement methods</td><td>Inspection, testing, checklists, check sheets, statistical sampling</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Outputs</td><td>Quality reports, test & evaluation documents</td><td>QC measurements, verified deliverables</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Quality report:</b> quality issues, recommendations for corrective action, summary of QC findings.</p>\n  <h3>Deliverable journey</h3>\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Deliverables</span><em>→ Control Quality →</em><span>Verified (correct)</span><em>→ Validate Scope →</em><span>Accepted (customer signs)</span></div>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Bakery: Plan = set the recipe standard. Manage = make sure bakers follow it and audit the kitchen. Control = weigh and inspect each loaf. Validate Scope = customer accepts.</div>"},{"h":"Cost of Quality","b":"\n  <table><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Cost of Conformance (spent to AVOID failure)</th><th colspan=\"2\">Cost of Nonconformance (spent BECAUSE of failure)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Prevention</b><br>\"Build a quality product\"</td><td><b>Appraisal</b><br>\"Assess the quality\"</td><td><b>Internal failure</b><br>\"Found by the project\"</td><td><b>External failure</b><br>\"Found by the customer\"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Training, document processes, equipment, time to do it right</td><td>Testing, destructive testing loss, inspections</td><td>Rework, scrap</td><td>Liabilities, warranty work, lost business</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Quick classify:</b> Was there a defect? No → conformance (prevention if stopping defects, appraisal if checking for them). Yes → nonconformance (internal if the team found it, external if the customer did).</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">P-A-I-E: Prevent, Assess, Inside failure, External failure.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Testing is an <b>appraisal</b> cost = conformance, even though it finds defects.</div>"},{"h":"Fishbone, Pareto, benchmarking, checklists","b":"\n  <p><b>Cause-and-effect (fishbone / Ishikawa) diagram</b> — a decomposition technique that traces an undesirable effect back to its <b>root cause</b>. Lecturer's example effect: \"Product quality not matching requirements\". Bones: <b>People</b> (fatigue, lack of training), <b>Process</b> (not enough R&D), <b>Equipment</b> (low maintenance), <b>Material</b> (low-quality raw material), <b>Environment</b> (bad working conditions), <b>Management</b> (low commitment to quality).</p>\n  <p><b>Pareto chart</b> — used in quality control to show the <b>most frequently occurring defects by category</b>; bars highest first (80/20 rule) → fix the vital few first.</p>\n  <p><b>Benchmarking</b> — comparing actual or planned products, processes and practices with those of <b>comparable organizations</b> to identify best practices and improvement ideas.</p>\n  <p><b>Checklists</b> — a list of items, actions or points to be considered; helps carry out quality control in a structured way.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Fishbone = WHY (causes). Pareto = HOW OFTEN (frequency).</div>"},{"h":"Six Sigma & DMAIC","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Six Sigma</b>A quality control methodology used to eliminate waste and defects in products and services and improve processes, using a step-by-step approach called DMAIC. Achieved when long-term defects are below <b>3.4 defects per million opportunities</b>.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th><th>Example (failed payments)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>D</b>efine</td><td>Choose a process; define the problem</td><td>\"Too many failed online payments\"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>M</b>easure</td><td>Measure the current process; collect data (benchmark); list inputs hindering performance</td><td>4% of payments fail</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>A</b>nalyze</td><td>Isolate and test each input as the possible root cause</td><td>Gateway timeouts at peak hours</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>I</b>mprove</td><td>Implement changes to improve performance</td><td>Add retry + second gateway</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>C</b>ontrol</td><td>Add controls so the process doesn't regress</td><td>Monitoring dashboard + alerts</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>D</b>on't <b>M</b>ake <b>A</b>ny <b>I</b>ncorrect <b>C</b>hanges.\"</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">3.4 per MILLION, not 3.4%.</div>"}],"recall":[["Define quality (PMBOK).","The degree to which a set of inherent characteristics fulfils requirements."],["3 quality management processes + their groups?","Plan Quality Management (Planning), Manage Quality (Executing), Control Quality (Monitoring & Controlling)."],["2 outputs of Plan Quality Management?","Quality management plan and quality metrics."],["Manage Quality focuses on ___; Control Quality focuses on ___.","Process; product."],["Which process produces verified deliverables? Where do they go next?","Control Quality. They go to Validate Scope, which produces accepted deliverables."],["4 categories of cost of quality?","Prevention and appraisal (conformance); internal failure and external failure (nonconformance)."],["Warranty work is which cost?","External failure (nonconformance)."],["What does a Pareto chart show?","The most frequently occurring defects by category."],["What does a fishbone diagram do?","Traces an undesirable effect back to its root cause; also called cause-and-effect or Ishikawa diagram."],["DMAIC stands for?","Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control."],["Six Sigma defect level?","Fewer than 3.4 defects per million opportunities."],["Define benchmarking.","Comparing actual or planned products, processes and practices with those of comparable organizations to identify best practices and ideas for improvement."]],"theory":[{"q":"Define quality and explain the three project quality management processes.","pts":["Quality definition (degree, inherent characteristics, fulfils requirements)","Plan Quality Mgmt: definition + plan & metrics","Manage Quality: definition + reports, test & evaluation docs","Control Quality: definition + QC measurements, verified deliverables","Process groups mentioned","Links verified → Validate Scope"],"model":"<p><b>Quality</b> is the degree to which a set of inherent characteristics fulfils requirements. Project Quality Management covers planning, managing and controlling project and product quality requirements.</p><ol><li><b>Plan Quality Management</b> (Planning) — identifies quality requirements and standards and documents how compliance will be demonstrated. Outputs: quality management plan and quality metrics.</li><li><b>Manage Quality</b> (Executing) — translates the quality management plan into executable quality activities that apply the organization's quality policies. Focuses on the process; uses audits. Outputs: quality reports, test and evaluation documents, change requests.</li><li><b>Control Quality</b> (Monitoring & Controlling) — monitors and records results of quality activities to ensure outputs are complete, correct and meet customer expectations. Focuses on the product; uses inspection and testing. Outputs: quality control measurements, verified deliverables, work performance information, change requests.</li></ol><p>Verified deliverables then go to Validate Scope for formal acceptance.</p>"},{"q":"Explain the cost of quality, giving examples of each category.","pts":["CoQ = conformance + nonconformance","Prevention + examples","Appraisal + examples","Internal failure + examples (found by project)","External failure + examples (found by customer)","Insight: prevention cheaper than failure"],"model":"<p><b>Cost of quality</b> is the total cost of conformance (money spent to avoid failures) and nonconformance (money spent because of failures).</p><p><b>Cost of conformance:</b></p><ul><li><b>Prevention costs</b> — building a quality product: training, documenting processes, equipment, time to do it right.</li><li><b>Appraisal costs</b> — assessing quality: testing, destructive testing loss, inspections.</li></ul><p><b>Cost of nonconformance:</b></p><ul><li><b>Internal failure costs</b> — failures found by the project: rework, scrap.</li><li><b>External failure costs</b> — failures found by the customer: liabilities, warranty work, lost business.</li></ul><p>Spending on prevention and appraisal reduces the much higher cost of failures, especially external ones that damage reputation.</p>"},{"q":"Describe Six Sigma and the DMAIC methodology.","pts":["Six Sigma definition (eliminate waste and defects, improve processes)","3.4 defects per million opportunities","Define explained","Measure explained","Analyze explained","Improve explained","Control explained"],"model":"<p><b>Six Sigma</b> is a quality control methodology used to eliminate waste and defects and improve processes. Six Sigma quality is reached when long-term defects are below 3.4 per million opportunities. It uses <b>DMAIC</b>:</p><ol><li><b>Define</b> — choose a process and define the problem to solve.</li><li><b>Measure</b> — measure key aspects of the current process, collect data as a benchmark, and list inputs that may be hindering performance.</li><li><b>Analyze</b> — isolate each input and test it as a possible root cause; establish cause and effect.</li><li><b>Improve</b> — implement changes that improve performance.</li><li><b>Control</b> — add controls so the process does not regress.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Distinguish between Manage Quality and Control Quality.","pts":["Manage Quality definition","Control Quality definition","Process focus vs product focus","Executing vs Monitoring & Controlling","Outputs of each","Tools: audits vs inspection/testing","Two-way link (QC measurements → MQ; T&E documents → CQ)"],"model":"<table><tr><th>Manage Quality</th><th>Control Quality</th></tr><tr><td>Translates the quality management plan into executable quality activities.</td><td>Monitors and records results of quality activities to make sure outputs are complete, correct and meet expectations.</td></tr><tr><td>Process-focused (quality assurance).</td><td>Product-focused (quality control).</td></tr><tr><td>Executing process group.</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling process group.</td></tr><tr><td>Tools: audits, process analysis, root cause analysis.</td><td>Tools: inspection, testing, checklists, check sheets.</td></tr><tr><td>Outputs: quality reports, test and evaluation documents.</td><td>Outputs: QC measurements, verified deliverables.</td></tr></table><p>They feed each other: Manage Quality uses Control Quality's measurements; Control Quality uses Manage Quality's test and evaluation documents.</p>"}]}],"calc":[{"t":"CPM","q":"Activities: A 3 days (start) · B 4 days (after A) · C 2 days (after A) · D 5 days (after B) · E 3 days (after C) · F 2 days (after D and E).<br><b>(a)</b> What is the project duration in days?","ans":14,"tol":0.01,"sol":"Paths: A–B–D–F = 3+4+5+2 = <b>14</b>; A–C–E–F = 3+2+3+2 = 10. Longest = 14 days → critical path A–B–D–F."},{"t":"CPM","q":"Same network as above. <b>(b)</b> How many days of float does activity C have?","ans":4,"tol":0.01,"sol":"C is on the non-critical path A–C–E–F (10 days). Float = 14 − 10 = <b>4 days</b>."},{"t":"PERT","q":"An activity has O = 4, M = 6, P = 14 days. What is its PERT expected duration?","ans":7,"tol":0.01,"sol":"E = (4 + 4×6 + 14) / 6 = 42 / 6 = <b>7 days</b>."},{"t":"PERT","q":"O = 3, M = 5, P = 10. Expected duration? (1 decimal place)","ans":5.5,"tol":0.06,"sol":"E = (3 + 20 + 10) / 6 = 33 / 6 = <b>5.5 days</b>."},{"t":"EMV","q":"Option X costs ₦5m with a 20% chance of a ₦15m penalty. What is the EMV of Option X in ₦m? (type the number; sign optional)","ans":-8,"tol":0.01,"abs":true,"sol":"Delay: 0.2 × −(5+15) = −4. No delay: 0.8 × −5 = −4. EMV = <b>−₦8m</b>."},{"t":"EMV","q":"Option Y costs ₦7m with a 5% chance of the same ₦15m penalty. EMV of Y in ₦m? (2 decimals)","ans":-7.75,"tol":0.01,"abs":true,"sol":"0.05 × −22 = −1.1; 0.95 × −7 = −6.65. EMV = <b>−₦7.75m</b>. Y has the lower expected cost, so choose Y over X (−8)."},{"t":"Budget","q":"Work package estimates total ₦40m. Contingency reserve ₦5m. Management reserve ₦3m. What is the cost baseline in ₦m?","ans":45,"tol":0.01,"sol":"Cost baseline = estimates + contingency = 40 + 5 = <b>₦45m</b> (management reserve excluded)."},{"t":"Budget","q":"Same figures. What is the project budget in ₦m?","ans":48,"tol":0.01,"sol":"Budget = cost baseline + management reserve = 45 + 3 = <b>₦48m</b>."},{"t":"P×I","q":"Probability 0.5, impact 0.4. What is the risk score?","ans":0.2,"tol":0.001,"sol":"0.5 × 0.4 = <b>0.20</b> → medium (yellow) on the lecturer's matrix."},{"t":"P×I","q":"Probability 0.3, impact 0.8. Risk score?","ans":0.24,"tol":0.001,"sol":"0.3 × 0.8 = <b>0.24</b> → high (red). Low chance but very high impact still ranks as high risk."}]};
